{"parentUuid":"879aaadd-f67a-4aa4-950d-2f114082f260","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"76000644-3315-41ac-95ed-c508ea0016f3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A93Hm6xhrdMo6q6aCuu3kB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# ADR-002: PageIndex Vectorless Spine — a Deterministic Document Backbone for Triple Fan-out and Cross-Document Reasoning\n2\t\n3\t| Field      | Value                                                                 |\n4\t| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n5\t| Status     | Proposed                                                              |\n6\t| Date       | 2026-06-23                                                            |\n7\t| Authors    | Binary 16 (engineering authority)                                     |\n8\t| Supersedes | —                                                                     |\n9\t| Related    | ADR-001 (determinism boundary), PIX-001 (docs/requirements/12), [VectifyAI/PageIndex](https://github.com/VectifyAI/PageIndex) |\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## 1. Context\n14\t\n15\tBenny's document surface is RAG-first: `/rag/ingest` extracts text, splits it on\n16\tblank lines, and upserts flat chunks into a per-workspace ChromaDB collection\n17\t(`benny/api/rag_routes.py`). Two structural weaknesses follow from this design,\n18\tand both are visible in the **shipped distribution**, not just in theory.\n19\t\n20\t**Observed state — `prime_silo_self` workspace (latest distribution):**\n21\t\n22\t- Four source docs (`GUIDE/README/ROADMAP/USER_GUIDE.md`, ~60 KB total).\n23\t- One successful ingest run (`306a222c`) → 48 flat chunks → ChromaDB (1.8 MB).\n24\t- Four of five ingest runs **failed** outright (\"No supported files found\").\n25\t- `reports/` empty, `.benny/wiki/` empty, **zero triples, zero Section graph.**\n26\t  `deep_synthesis` was off, so nothing reached Neo4j. The knowledge graph the\n27\t  Notebook is meant to render is, in the real product, empty.\n28\t\n29\t**Weakness 1 — the embedding provider is a single point of failure.** The\n30\tvector path requires a live local embedding server (LM Studio / Lemonade /\n31\tOllama). When it is down, upserts silently store zero-vectors; an entire class\n32\tof remediation code exists only because of this (`get_embedding_sync(\"ping\")`\n33\tpreflight, `heal_collection_dimension`, the 768-vs-384 dimension fix). For a\n34\tzero-install desktop EXE this is the dominant ingestion failure mode.\n35\t\n36\t**Weakness 2 — the triple fan-out partition is arbitrary and truncated.** When\n37\t`deep_synthesis` *does* run, `parallel_extract_triples` is fed\n38\t`chunks[:10]` — blank-line paragraph splits, hard-capped at 10, titled\n39\t\"Part 1/2/3…\". The partition is non-reproducible and silently drops everything\n40\tpast the 10th paragraph. Triples are anchored only to a **filename**\n41\t(`save_knowledge_triples(workspace, triples, file_path.name)`), so there is no\n42\tsection/page provenance, and cross-document linking falls back to concept-\n43\tembedding cosine similarity (`run_full_correlation_suite`, threshold 0.82) —\n44\tvectors again.\n45\t\n46\tPageIndex ([VectifyAI](https://github.com/VectifyAI/PageIndex)) proposes a\n47\t**vectorless, reasoning-based** index: a document becomes a hierarchical tree\n48\t(a table-of-contents of titled nodes with summaries and page ranges), and\n49\tretrieval is LLM tree-traversal rather than vector similarity. The user's\n50\tinsight is to use that tree not merely for retrieval but as a **deterministic\n51\tspine**: build the abstract first, load it into the graph for the human to see\n52\tand the agent to traverse, then fan small extractor agents out over the tree's\n53\treal sections so the parts compose into a connected whole.\n54\t\n55\t## 2. Decision\n56\t\n57\tAdopt the PageIndex tree as a **parallel ingestion strategy and a structural\n58\tbackbone for synthesis** — not a replacement for vector RAG, and not (in this\n59\tADR) an agentic multi-hop retriever. Specifically:\n60\t\n61\t1. **Introduce a pure spine module** `benny/core/pageindex.py` holding the\n62\t   indexed-abstract tree and the deterministic operations over it\n63\t   (`flatten_leaves`, `tree_to_sections`, `abstract_outline`,\n64\t   `build_section_edges`, `validate_tree`). It contains **no LLM call and no\n65\t   Neo4j driver**, so completeness/provenance/reproducibility are unit-testable\n66\t   offline. (Phase 0 — shipped with this ADR.)\n67\t\n68\t2. **Build the tree at ingest** from the existing extracted text via\n69\t   `call_model()` (ADR-001 rule: never bypass the model router). Persist it to\n70\t   `workspace/.benny/pageindex/<source>.json` and as a Neo4j `Section`\n71\t   hierarchy: `(:Document)-[:HAS_SECTION]->(:Section)-[:HAS_SECTION]->...`.\n72\t\n73\t3. **Fan triple extraction out over tree leaves, not `chunks[:10]`.**\n74\t   `tree_to_sections(tree)` yields exactly the `[{node_id, title, text}]` shape\n75\t   `parallel_extract_triples` already accepts — a clean drop-in with no\n76\t   signature change — and covers the whole document with no cap.\n77\t\n78\t4. **Anchor triples to their Section** (node_id + page range), giving every\n79\t   extracted relationship section/page provenance instead of just a filename.\n80\t\n81\t5. **Add a cheap cross-document pass** over section *summaries* (the abstract\n82\t   layer) to propose candidate `Section`↔`Section` links across documents\n83\t   *before* spending full-text LLM calls — the \"greater than the sum of the\n84\t   parts\" step.\n85\t\n86\t6. **Add a `structured` retrieval route** to the Adaptive RAG router\n87\t   (`benny/core/adaptive_rag.py`) that, for tree-backed documents, does a\n88\t   **single** node-selection call over `abstract_outline(tree)` then loads the\n89\t   selected leaves — explicitly *not* an N-hop agentic traversal (see §4).\n90\t\n91\t## 3. Why this fits the determinism boundary (ADR-001)\n92\t\n93\tThe spine slots into the existing two-zone model rather than fighting it.\n94\t\n95\t| Concern                | Spine behaviour                                                                 |\n96\t| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n97\t| Deterministic fan-out  | Same document → same tree (temperature 0) → same leaf set → same triple work units. Reproducible by construction. |\n98\t| Provenance / audit     | Triples carry `node_id` + page range; every Section write is a lineage event, matching the \"agent authorship is auditable\" stance of ADR-001 §5. |\n99\t| Vectorless path        | Tree build + node-select use `call_model()`; **no embedding server**, so the entire zero-vector failure class disappears for tree-backed docs. |\n100\t| Coexistence            | Vector RAG remains the default; `structured` is a router choice per query. No existing collection or route is removed. |\n101\t\n102\t## 4. The local-NPU constraint (and why retrieval stays single-call)\n103\t\n104\t`adaptive_rag.py` already documents that agentic, multi-call loops on a local\n105\t12B NPU model \"run for minutes\" and drop the caller's connection — which is why\n106\t`self_check=False` is the default lean pipeline. PageIndex's reference retriever\n107\tis agentic tree traversal (many reasoning calls); ported naively it reintroduces\n108\texactly that latency.\n109\t\n110\t**Resolution:** the tree's abstract (titles + summaries only) is small and fits\n111\tin context, so retrieval is **one** node-selection call + generation — two LLM\n112\tround-trips total, matching the lean pipeline. The expensive tree *construction*\n113\tis one-time at ingest, batchable and async, off the request path.\n114\t\n115\t## 5. Consequences\n116\t\n117\t### Positive\n118\t\n119\t- **Populates a graph that is currently empty** in the shipped distribution.\n120\t- **Kills the embedding-server failure class** for tree-backed documents.\n121\t- **Deterministic, complete triple fan-out** — removes the `chunks[:10]` cap and\n122\t  the arbitrary partition; reproducible under the SR-1 / replay gates.\n123\t- **Section/page provenance** for every triple; cross-doc links proposed from a\n124\t  cheap summary layer instead of concept-embedding cosine.\n125\t- **A human-navigable abstract** (the indexed table-of-contents) that doubles as\n126\t  the agent's cheap planning map.\n127\t\n128\t### Negative\n129\t\n130\t- **Tree build is LLM-bound** at ingest; slow on a local NPU for large corpora\n131\t  (mitigated: one-time, async, batched).\n132\t- **Second index to maintain** alongside ChromaDB for tree-backed docs.\n133\t- **Quality depends on the tree builder** — a bad tree yields a bad partition.\n134\t  Mitigated by `validate_tree` invariants and a deterministic re-build.\n135\t\n136\t### Neutral\n137\t\n138\t- Vector RAG, `single_step`/`multi_hop` routes, and the existing collections are\n139\t  untouched. The spine is additive.\n140\t\n141\t## 6. Alternatives considered\n142\t\n143\t- **Replace vector RAG wholesale with PageIndex.** Rejected — vector recall is\n144\t  genuinely better for fuzzy lookups across many small notes; PageIndex wins on\n145\t  long *structured* docs. Hybrid is what PageIndex itself recommends.\n146\t- **Vendor the upstream PageIndex library directly.** Rejected for the LLM half\n147\t  — it is LiteLLM/OpenAI-wired and would bypass `call_model()` (ADR-001 rule 1,\n148\t  offline mode, lineage). We port its prompts onto the router instead; PDF\n149\t  parsing we already own (`fitz` / Docling).\n150\t- **Keep `chunks[:10]` but raise the cap.** Rejected — still arbitrary, still\n151\t  non-reproducible, still no provenance.\n152\t\n153\t## 7. Open questions\n154\t\n155\t1. **Tree-builder model + token budget per node** under offline mode (local\n156\t   reasoning model vs. cloud). → PIX open_questions OQ-1.\n157\t2. **`Section` schema reconciliation** with the existing `Concept`/`Document`\n158\t   knowledge-graph nodes and the enrichment `CORRELATES_WITH` overlay. → OQ-2.\n159\t3. **Cross-document candidate-link threshold** on summaries — reasoning call vs.\n160\t   cheap clustering. → OQ-3.\n161\t4. **Trigger policy** — auto-detect \"structured\" docs (PDF with real TOC) vs. an\n162\t   explicit `strategy: \"vectorless\"` ingest flag. → OQ-4.\n163\t\n164\t---\n165\t\n166\t*ADR-002 — PageIndex vectorless spine — Binary 16 — for review. Phase 0\n167\t(pure spine module + tests) shipped with this ADR; Phases 1–4 tracked in\n168\t[docs/requirements/12/project_plan.md](../docs/requirements/12/project_plan.md).*\n169\t"}]},"uuid":"800909d4-1d3c-4979-a353-e27f8a783824","timestamp":"2026-06-28T15:19:23.891Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nsdha\\OneDrive\\binary16\\prime-silo\\runtime\\architecture\\ADR-002-pageindex-vectorless-spine.md","content":"# ADR-002: PageIndex Vectorless Spine — a Deterministic Document Backbone for Triple Fan-out and Cross-Document Reasoning\n\n| Field      | Value                                                                 |\n| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Status     | Proposed                                                              |\n| Date       | 2026-06-23                                                            |\n| Authors    | Binary 16 (engineering authority)                                     |\n| Supersedes | —                                                                     |\n| Related    | ADR-001 (determinism boundary), PIX-001 (docs/requirements/12), [VectifyAI/PageIndex](https://github.com/VectifyAI/PageIndex) |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Context\n\nBenny's document surface is RAG-first: `/rag/ingest` extracts text, splits it on\nblank lines, and upserts flat chunks into a per-workspace ChromaDB collection\n(`benny/api/rag_routes.py`). Two structural weaknesses follow from this design,\nand both are visible in the **shipped distribution**, not just in theory.\n\n**Observed state — `prime_silo_self` workspace (latest distribution):**\n\n- Four source docs (`GUIDE/README/ROADMAP/USER_GUIDE.md`, ~60 KB total).\n- One successful ingest run (`306a222c`) → 48 flat chunks → ChromaDB (1.8 MB).\n- Four of five ingest runs **failed** outright (\"No supported files found\").\n- `reports/` empty, `.benny/wiki/` empty, **zero triples, zero Section graph.**\n  `deep_synthesis` was off, so nothing reached Neo4j. The knowledge graph the\n  Notebook is meant to render is, in the real product, empty.\n\n**Weakness 1 — the embedding provider is a single point of failure.** The\nvector path requires a live local embedding server (LM Studio / Lemonade /\nOllama). When it is down, upserts silently store zero-vectors; an entire class\nof remediation code exists only because of this (`get_embedding_sync(\"ping\")`\npreflight, `heal_collection_dimension`, the 768-vs-384 dimension fix). For a\nzero-install desktop EXE this is the dominant ingestion failure mode.\n\n**Weakness 2 — the triple fan-out partition is arbitrary and truncated.** When\n`deep_synthesis` *does* run, `parallel_extract_triples` is fed\n`chunks[:10]` — blank-line paragraph splits, hard-capped at 10, titled\n\"Part 1/2/3…\". The partition is non-reproducible and silently drops everything\npast the 10th paragraph. Triples are anchored only to a **filename**\n(`save_knowledge_triples(workspace, triples, file_path.name)`), so there is no\nsection/page provenance, and cross-document linking falls back to concept-\nembedding cosine similarity (`run_full_correlation_suite`, threshold 0.82) —\nvectors again.\n\nPageIndex ([VectifyAI](https://github.com/VectifyAI/PageIndex)) proposes a\n**vectorless, reasoning-based** index: a document becomes a hierarchical tree\n(a table-of-contents of titled nodes with summaries and page ranges), and\nretrieval is LLM tree-traversal rather than vector similarity. The user's\ninsight is to use that tree not merely for retrieval but as a **deterministic\nspine**: build the abstract first, load it into the graph for the human to see\nand the agent to traverse, then fan small extractor agents out over the tree's\nreal sections so the parts compose into a connected whole.\n\n## 2. Decision\n\nAdopt the PageIndex tree as a **parallel ingestion strategy and a structural\nbackbone for synthesis** — not a replacement for vector RAG, and not (in this\nADR) an agentic multi-hop retriever. Specifically:\n\n1. **Introduce a pure spine module** `benny/core/pageindex.py` holding the\n   indexed-abstract tree and the deterministic operations over it\n   (`flatten_leaves`, `tree_to_sections`, `abstract_outline`,\n   `build_section_edges`, `validate_tree`). It contains **no LLM call and no\n   Neo4j driver**, so completeness/provenance/reproducibility are unit-testable\n   offline. (Phase 0 — shipped with this ADR.)\n\n2. **Build the tree at ingest** from the existing extracted text via\n   `call_model()` (ADR-001 rule: never bypass the model router). Persist it to\n   `workspace/.benny/pageindex/<source>.json` and as a Neo4j `Section`\n   hierarchy: `(:Document)-[:HAS_SECTION]->(:Section)-[:HAS_SECTION]->...`.\n\n3. **Fan triple extraction out over tree leaves, not `chunks[:10]`.**\n   `tree_to_sections(tree)` yields exactly the `[{node_id, title, text}]` shape\n   `parallel_extract_triples` already accepts — a clean drop-in with no\n   signature change — and covers the whole document with no cap.\n\n4. **Anchor triples to their Section** (node_id + page range), giving every\n   extracted relationship section/page provenance instead of just a filename.\n\n5. **Add a cheap cross-document pass** over section *summaries* (the abstract\n   layer) to propose candidate `Section`↔`Section` links across documents\n   *before* spending full-text LLM calls — the \"greater than the sum of the\n   parts\" step.\n\n6. **Add a `structured` retrieval route** to the Adaptive RAG router\n   (`benny/core/adaptive_rag.py`) that, for tree-backed documents, does a\n   **single** node-selection call over `abstract_outline(tree)` then loads the\n   selected leaves — explicitly *not* an N-hop agentic traversal (see §4).\n\n## 3. Why this fits the determinism boundary (ADR-001)\n\nThe spine slots into the existing two-zone model rather than fighting it.\n\n| Concern                | Spine behaviour                                                                 |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Deterministic fan-out  | Same document → same tree (temperature 0) → same leaf set → same triple work units. Reproducible by construction. |\n| Provenance / audit     | Triples carry `node_id` + page range; every Section write is a lineage event, matching the \"agent authorship is auditable\" stance of ADR-001 §5. |\n| Vectorless path        | Tree build + node-select use `call_model()`; **no embedding server**, so the entire zero-vector failure class disappears for tree-backed docs. |\n| Coexistence            | Vector RAG remains the default; `structured` is a router choice per query. No existing collection or route is removed. |\n\n## 4. The local-NPU constraint (and why retrieval stays single-call)\n\n`adaptive_rag.py` already documents that agentic, multi-call loops on a local\n12B NPU model \"run for minutes\" and drop the caller's connection — which is why\n`self_check=False` is the default lean pipeline. PageIndex's reference retriever\nis agentic tree traversal (many reasoning calls); ported naively it reintroduces\nexactly that latency.\n\n**Resolution:** the tree's abstract (titles + summaries only) is small and fits\nin context, so retrieval is **one** node-selection call + generation — two LLM\nround-trips total, matching the lean pipeline. The expensive tree *construction*\nis one-time at ingest, batchable and async, off the request path.\n\n## 5. Consequences\n\n### Positive\n\n- **Populates a graph that is currently empty** in the shipped distribution.\n- **Kills the embedding-server failure class** for tree-backed documents.\n- **Deterministic, complete triple fan-out** — removes the `chunks[:10]` cap and\n  the arbitrary partition; reproducible under the SR-1 / replay gates.\n- **Section/page provenance** for every triple; cross-doc links proposed from a\n  cheap summary layer instead of concept-embedding cosine.\n- **A human-navigable abstract** (the indexed table-of-contents) that doubles as\n  the agent's cheap planning map.\n\n### Negative\n\n- **Tree build is LLM-bound** at ingest; slow on a local NPU for large corpora\n  (mitigated: one-time, async, batched).\n- **Second index to maintain** alongside ChromaDB for tree-backed docs.\n- **Quality depends on the tree builder** — a bad tree yields a bad partition.\n  Mitigated by `validate_tree` invariants and a deterministic re-build.\n\n### Neutral\n\n- Vector RAG, `single_step`/`multi_hop` routes, and the existing collections are\n  untouched. The spine is additive.\n\n## 6. Alternatives considered\n\n- **Replace vector RAG wholesale with PageIndex.** Rejected — vector recall is\n  genuinely better for fuzzy lookups across many small notes; PageIndex wins on\n  long *structured* docs. Hybrid is what PageIndex itself recommends.\n- **Vendor the upstream PageIndex library directly.** Rejected for the LLM half\n  — it is LiteLLM/OpenAI-wired and would bypass `call_model()` (ADR-001 rule 1,\n  offline mode, lineage). We port its prompts onto the router instead; PDF\n  parsing we already own (`fitz` / Docling).\n- **Keep `chunks[:10]` but raise the cap.** Rejected — still arbitrary, still\n  non-reproducible, still no provenance.\n\n## 7. Open questions\n\n1. **Tree-builder model + token budget per node** under offline mode (local\n   reasoning model vs. cloud). → PIX open_questions OQ-1.\n2. **`Section` schema reconciliation** with the existing `Concept`/`Document`\n   knowledge-graph nodes and the